WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.971 --> 00:00:06.051
What’s up, Wisecrack?

00:00:06.051 --> 00:00:07.051
Helen again.

00:00:07.051 --> 00:00:12.201
This summer, audiences got an arguably unnecessary
addition to one of cinema’s most perfect

00:00:12.201 --> 00:00:14.561
trilogies with Toy Story 4.

00:00:14.561 --> 00:00:18.971
The original three films about a band of spunky,
sentient children’s playthings grappled

00:00:18.971 --> 00:00:24.601
with surprisingly deep questions about love,
loss, identity, being forgotten, moving on

00:00:24.601 --> 00:00:25.671
and more.

00:00:25.671 --> 00:00:29.861
This was a pinnacle achievement in children’s
cinema - in which sequels often feel like

00:00:29.861 --> 00:00:32.171
clumsy, narratively gratuitous cash grabs.

00:00:32.171 --> 00:00:37.021
So we went into this fourth film wondering
- is there really anything left for the series

00:00:37.021 --> 00:00:40.351
to say, or are we just pandering to Randy
Newman fans at this point?

00:00:40.351 --> 00:01:26.861
Let’s investigate in this Wisecrack Edition
on Toy Story 4: Deep or Dumb?

00:01:26.861 --> 00:02:19.741
And, of course, spoilers ahead.

00:02:19.741 --> 00:02:22.051
Alright guys, let’s dive into a recap.

00:02:22.051 --> 00:02:26.881
Toy Story 4 starts with a flashback to the
young Andy days, as Woody watches Bo Peep

00:02:26.881 --> 00:02:28.131
being given away.

00:02:28.131 --> 00:02:32.700
She asks him to come with her, but he decides
“bros before Bo’s” and stays with Andy.

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:36.971
Flash forward to teenage Andy giving Woody
and co. to a hyperactive kid named Bonnie.

00:02:36.971 --> 00:02:41.491
Even as Bonnie becomes increasingly less interested
in Woody, his loyalty leads him to follow

00:02:41.491 --> 00:02:45.670
her to kindergarten, where he helps her create
Forky, which is exactly what it sounds like:

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.951
A spork with mismatched googly-eyes and pipe
cleaner arms.

00:02:48.951 --> 00:02:53.741
Bonnie effing loves Forky, but Forky thinks
he’s trash and keeps trying end it all in

00:02:53.741 --> 00:02:55.161
the nearest rubbish bin.

00:02:55.161 --> 00:02:59.451
Woody has to prevent Forky from trashing himself
when Bonnie’s family heads out on an RV

00:02:59.451 --> 00:03:00.451
trip.

00:03:00.451 --> 00:03:04.611
Mishaps ensue, and Forky is promptly kidnapped
in an antique store by a sinister doll, Gabby

00:03:04.611 --> 00:03:07.491
Gabby and her horrifying marionette minions.

00:03:07.491 --> 00:03:11.670
Lost on a playground, a hapless Woody runs
into his old boo, Bo Peep, who is stanning

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:13.191
the “lost toy” lifestyle.

00:03:13.191 --> 00:03:17.310
Together, the gang sets out on an elaborate
quest to rescue Bonnie’s beloved plastic

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:18.890
spork from Gabby Gabby.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:23.521
Only it turns out Gabby Gabby just wants Woody’s
voice box to replace her defective one so

00:03:23.521 --> 00:03:25.981
she can convince a little girl to love her.

00:03:25.981 --> 00:03:29.941
Woody agrees to give up his voice box, the
marionette performs some quick black market

00:03:29.941 --> 00:03:33.321
surgery, and, in exchange, Woody gets Forky
back.

00:03:33.321 --> 00:03:37.221
Everything culminates at the carnival grounds
where Woody finds his calling as a Lost Toy

00:03:37.221 --> 00:03:39.941
and decides that, this time, he’ll stay
with Bo.

00:03:39.941 --> 00:03:43.420
They then devote themselves to helping toys
find their own way.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:44.420
The end.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:48.200
Now, Pixar is known for making some of the
best written and most thoughtful films out

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:50.991
there, and the Toy Story movies are no exception.

00:03:50.991 --> 00:03:54.890
So we think it’s worth asking if Woody’s
emotional journey is about more than regretting

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:56.281
a breakup with a hot shepard.

00:03:56.281 --> 00:04:00.140
We’ve come up with three possible interpretations
of the film to run by you guys.

00:04:00.140 --> 00:04:04.081
Our first argument: Toy Story 4 is a tale
of existential awakening.

00:04:04.081 --> 00:04:05.081
Briefly, Existentialism is a philosophy that
emphasizes individual free will and our power

00:04:05.081 --> 00:04:06.081
to make choices that determine the course
of our lives.

00:04:06.081 --> 00:04:08.780
The first three films have brushed against
existential themes, asking what it means to

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:12.161
be a toy who loves and is loved, or no longer
loved.

00:04:12.161 --> 00:04:14.550
But in this fourth film it really goes there.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:18.610
See, nearly every character in the film has
some kind of conflict with their “inner

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:21.001
voice” that governs their decision making
process.

00:04:21.001 --> 00:04:29.930
Buzz struggles to make sense of his factory-issued
dialogue, while Woody grapples with his own

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:33.300
conscience to parse what he should do from
what he wants to do.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:37.920
Meanwhile, Forky’s poor, plastic spork brain
insists that he is trash, and Gabby Gabby

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:41.851
longs to fix her voice box so she can speak
the words her toymakers intended her to.

00:04:41.851 --> 00:04:46.180
To grasp how the inner voice speaks to a key
element of existentialism, we’re looking

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:50.151
to one bespectacled French philosopher Jean-Paul
Sartre.

00:04:50.151 --> 00:04:54.911
Sartre believed that we are all radically
free and, because of that, it's up to us to

00:04:54.911 --> 00:04:56.890
determine who we become based on our choices.

00:04:56.890 --> 00:05:01.471
YOU choose the path you walk, the ice cream
flavor you eat, and the YouTube videos you

00:05:01.471 --> 00:05:03.210
watch, algorithm be damned!

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:09.231
In his essay “Existentialism is a Humanism”,
Sartre famously wrote that “existence precedes

00:05:09.231 --> 00:05:14.330
essence,” explaining that “first of all
man exists, turns up, appears on the scene,

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:16.550
and only afterwards, defines himself.”

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:19.360
Basically, there is no “essence” that
defines you from birth.

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.850
You first exist, then your essence is defined
by your choices.

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:26.700
You aren’t born the kind of person who wears
pleated khakis for fun, you become that when

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:29.040
you choose to wear your school uniform to
parties.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.070
These choices define your character.

00:05:31.070 --> 00:05:34.881
In this way, we can see Bo Peep as the consummate
existentialist.

00:05:34.881 --> 00:05:39.880
She’s broken free from her obligations as
a toy belonging to children, and no longer

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:42.700
feels compelled to live by any outside creed.

00:05:42.700 --> 00:05:47.700
This separation from the rules and expectations
of her past is symbolized in her freeing herself

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:49.240
from the lamp that she came with.

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:53.540
She runs amuck, exploring the world and playing
with whatever kids she wants.

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:57.841
Most importantly, she doesn’t blame anyone
for her choices - She is accountable to no

00:05:57.841 --> 00:06:00.720
one and holds no one accountable for herself.

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:02.031
She is free.

00:06:02.031 --> 00:06:06.781
The other characters are all struggling with
non-existential thinking - they define themselves

00:06:06.781 --> 00:06:11.621
not by the individual choices they make, but
by their essence as toys as defined by their

00:06:11.621 --> 00:06:13.621
quote unquote inner voice.

00:06:13.621 --> 00:06:16.531
This is apparent in Woody’s single-minded
devotion to Bonnie.

00:06:16.531 --> 00:06:20.710
He sees it as his duty to serve her, even
though she’s clearly no longer interested

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:21.710
in playing with him.

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:26.121
Regardless, he feels like he can’t make
the choice to be a toy without a kid.

00:06:26.121 --> 00:06:31.420
Similarly, Forky sees himself as essentially
made from trash, and thus identifies entirely

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:40.531
as said trash, rather than a toy.

00:06:40.531 --> 00:06:44.380
This line of thought is especially apparent
in the toys that were born “defective.”

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:48.680
For Gabby-Gabby, whose voice box doesn’t
work, her failure to fully embody her designed

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:53.291
functionality makes her sub par, and justifies
her sometimes-cruel actions.

00:06:53.291 --> 00:06:57.270
Or take Duke Kaboom, who, in not being able
to jump through his hoop, failed to live up

00:06:57.270 --> 00:07:00.200
to the impossible expectations set by his
commercials.

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:04.360
That failure, we see, has become an excuse
to do nothing with his life but weep and bemoan

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:05.360
what could have been.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:09.680
For these two characters, their inability
to embody an aspect of their supposed essence,

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:10.751
haunts them.

00:07:10.751 --> 00:07:14.740
They define themselves not by the choices
they make, but by a perceived lack with which

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:17.460
they were born, and thus are miserable.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:21.941
Now, because Sartre believed that the choices
we make determine our essence, he also was

00:07:21.941 --> 00:07:28.740
big on the idea of personal responsibility
- or as he puts it:

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:32.290
This might sound fun at first - don’t we
all want to be in control of our own lives

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:33.550
and eat ice cream for breakfast?

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:37.790
It’s not that simple though - because a
dude’s choices determine his destiny, he

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:40.031
“is responsible for everything he does.”

00:07:40.031 --> 00:07:43.670
So when you get cavities, you have nobody
to blame but yourself.

00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:48.121
As a result, Sartre doesn’t see freedom
as a fun walk in the park - in fact, he writes

00:07:48.121 --> 00:07:50.230
that “man is condemned to be free.”

00:07:50.230 --> 00:07:56.430
A lot of people find that freedom hard to
take, which is why, you know… cults.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:08:01.350
This conflict around accepting personal responsibility
for your choices is probably best embodied

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:02.670
by Buzz Lightyear.

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:06.460
Whenever he’s faced with a tough decision,
Buzz presses one of his buttons and waits

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:10.550
for his “programming” to issue a command
- calling this his “inner voice.”

00:08:10.550 --> 00:08:14.350
Rather than take responsibility for his choices,
he simply outsources them.

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:16.440
This can occasionally help him get out of
a jam:

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:17.480
“What would Woody do?”

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:19.311
“Eh, jump out of a moving vehicle.”

00:08:19.311 --> 00:08:21.151
“What would Woody do?”

00:08:21.151 --> 00:08:25.001
“There’s a secret mission, in uncharted
space, let’s go!”

00:08:25.001 --> 00:08:27.160
but doesn’t always yield the most helpful
advice:

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:28.160
“Scanning perimeter.”

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:31.401
“Lasers at full power-”
“Buzz, what are you doing?”

00:08:31.401 --> 00:08:32.401
“I’m thinking.”

00:08:32.401 --> 00:08:35.500
This is also reflected in Woody’s preoccupation
with his inner voice.

00:08:35.500 --> 00:08:40.361
While Buzz’s inner voice was literally manufactured
by a factory, Woody’s was developed after

00:08:40.361 --> 00:08:43.901
years of believing that a toy only exists
for the joy of a kid.

00:08:43.901 --> 00:08:48.380
As a result, he sees his duties as clearly
defined: He has to help Bonnie however he

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:49.380
can.

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:50.380
As he puts it:

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:54.731
“I need to do this that little voice inside
me would never leave me alone if I gave up.”

00:08:54.731 --> 00:08:57.170
This is despite the fact that Bonnie’s really
over him.

00:08:57.170 --> 00:09:01.620
Here, we could say that Woody is acting in
“bad faith” or what Sartre would refer

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:04.711
to as the state of pretending that you’re
not free.

00:09:04.711 --> 00:09:15.041
In a burn-happy quote, Sartre says:

00:09:15.041 --> 00:09:18.581
In the first scene of the film, Woody pretends
that he doesn’t have the freedom to leave

00:09:18.581 --> 00:09:21.261
Andy behind and run away with Bo Peep.

00:09:21.261 --> 00:09:25.201
Sartre would say that he does, and the only
thing that stopped him was his choice not

00:09:25.201 --> 00:09:26.201
to.

00:09:26.201 --> 00:09:30.231
Throughout the course of the film, we watch
as each character basically becomes…, through

00:09:30.231 --> 00:09:32.351
a Sartrean lens, kind of, really human?...

00:09:32.351 --> 00:09:33.351
coming to terms with their own autonomy and
freedom to make choices.

00:09:33.351 --> 00:09:34.351
That’s right, toys are resolving their existential
crises onscreen, what have you done today?

00:09:34.351 --> 00:09:38.430
Most importantly, Woody chooses to stay with
Bo Peep, thus embracing his radical freedom

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:43.000
and rejecting his supposed essential “purpose”
of being a child’s plaything.

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:48.391
Even though leaving his child behind once
seemed unfathomable, he realizes that he can,

00:09:48.391 --> 00:09:50.781
and so, he does.

00:09:50.781 --> 00:09:52.370
Existential crisis averted!

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:57.110
Importantly, Buzz too has his own free will
affirmed during a critical moment - He needs

00:09:57.110 --> 00:10:01.841
Bonnie to bring the family back to the antique
store to rescue Woody and Forky, but she just

00:10:01.841 --> 00:10:04.370
won’t notice that she left her backpack
behind.

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:07.570
After his buttons fail to guide him, he uses
his own voice:

00:10:07.570 --> 00:10:10.261
“Your backpack is in the antique store,
let’s go!”

00:10:10.261 --> 00:10:11.261
“Oh no!

00:10:11.261 --> 00:10:12.261
My backpack!”

00:10:12.261 --> 00:10:16.971
Here, Buzz recognizes that he can’t rely
on his factory-issued “inner voice,” and

00:10:16.971 --> 00:10:20.761
is forced to take action to save the day-
to make a choice.

00:10:20.761 --> 00:10:26.351
When we first meet GabbyGabby, she has an
almost religious attachment to her own mythos,

00:10:26.351 --> 00:10:30.791
reading her illustrated companion book as
if its a biblical text and treating tea-time

00:10:30.791 --> 00:10:34.851
with her inattentive “God”, Harmony, as
if it is a spiritual rite.

00:10:34.851 --> 00:10:39.841
Her life is defined by her desire to please
Harmony, and she goes to extraordinary lengths

00:10:39.841 --> 00:10:40.861
to do so.

00:10:40.861 --> 00:10:45.820
But then, even when her voice box is fixed,
she's still rejected by her, and the narrative

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:48.161
in the book is proven false.

00:10:48.161 --> 00:10:51.750
With the help of Woody and Bo, she learns
that she’s not at the mercy of the Gabby

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:56.621
Gabby mythos- that it’s up to her to define
herself and her worth through the choices

00:10:56.621 --> 00:10:57.621
she makes.

00:10:57.621 --> 00:11:02.031
Similarly, Sartre’s whole schtick is predicated
on the belief that God does not exist.

00:11:02.031 --> 00:11:05.930
Because there is no bearded dude in the sky
controlling our lives, it must be up to us

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:07.221
to choose our own path.

00:11:07.221 --> 00:11:11.630
When Gabby Gabby encounters the lost little
girl at the fair, she chooses to offer herself

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:12.711
up for adoption.

00:11:12.711 --> 00:11:16.511
Sure, she’s submitting to the ownership
model that Woody’s about to leave behind,

00:11:16.511 --> 00:11:19.261
but she’s openly choosing it over the alternative.

00:11:19.261 --> 00:11:23.920
Similarly, Ducky and Bunny, the lovable Key
and Peele carnival toys go from wanting to

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:29.301
passively be chosen by a child who will love
them to becoming super-active freedom fighters,

00:11:29.301 --> 00:11:33.180
busting other stuffed animals out of the carnival-booth
purgatory they loathed.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:38.701
Meanwhile, Duke Kaboom, at the urging of Woody
and Bo, chooses to leap on his motorcycle,

00:11:38.701 --> 00:11:43.110
first four feet then forty, in spite of the
fact that he won’t land perfectly like he’s

00:11:43.110 --> 00:11:44.110
supposed to in the commercial.

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:48.380
He’s learned to no longer live by the standards
dictated by his design.

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:50.870
That, friends, is called freedom.

00:11:50.870 --> 00:11:55.250
And we can’t forget Forky - easily the weirdest
character in the Toy Story canon.

00:11:55.250 --> 00:12:00.521
Early on, Forky identifies as trash - he was
made to be a spork, and should have wound

00:12:00.521 --> 00:12:05.990
up in the warm, warm embrace of the garbage:
“I am not a toy, I’m a spork!”

00:12:05.990 --> 00:12:06.990
“Be quiet!”

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:11.831
“ I was made for soup, salad, maybe chili,
and then the trash!

00:12:11.831 --> 00:12:13.980
I’m litter, freedom!”

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:18.351
However, with the help of Woody, he realizes
that he isn’t a slave to his inner voice

00:12:18.351 --> 00:12:22.250
- He can establish a new identity as a toy
who is loved by Bonnie.

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:27.491
He’s so thoroughly inculcated to his own
freedoms that, by the end of the film, he’s

00:12:27.491 --> 00:12:32.201
even prepared to pass on some of that existential
wisdom to his new love interest.

00:12:32.201 --> 00:12:35.201
“We are all toys.

00:12:35.201 --> 00:12:37.451
Unique, beautiful, toys.”

00:12:37.451 --> 00:12:41.721
Each character’s narrative arc shows them
grow from a character who accepts the destiny

00:12:41.721 --> 00:12:47.681
thrust upon them, to an almost eerily human-like
being who makes choices that have consequences,

00:12:47.681 --> 00:12:50.811
and thus exhibits autonomy over their own
existence.

00:12:50.811 --> 00:12:52.180
That’s pretty beautiful.

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:57.531
We’d grade this interpretation a cool A,
and say that, viewed through this lens, the

00:12:57.531 --> 00:12:59.091
film is deep with a capital D.

00:12:59.091 --> 00:13:03.661
So now that we’ve gotten the existential
mind-fuck out of the way, its time for our

00:13:03.661 --> 00:13:07.841
second interpretation: that Bo Peep is the
Miltonian Satan.

00:13:07.841 --> 00:13:08.930
I know.

00:13:08.930 --> 00:13:09.930
Hear us out.

00:13:09.930 --> 00:13:14.500
In the world of these toys, children are basically
God-like creatures who they spend their lives

00:13:14.500 --> 00:13:15.500
serving.

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:18.990
They live their lives for their children,
just as a religious person might live their

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:20.051
life for God.

00:13:20.051 --> 00:13:24.360
In fact, the god-liness of children in this
world is made pretty literal with the introduction

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:29.370
of Forky, an inanimate object who Bonnie,
through the sheer power of childish wonder,

00:13:29.370 --> 00:13:32.050
whimsy and a couple of googly-eyes, brings
to life.

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:36.421
It’s also worth noting the almost religious
reverence that the toys have for their children:

00:13:36.421 --> 00:13:37.721
“No, I can’t, sir.

00:13:37.721 --> 00:13:40.651
Bo, I need to get back to my kid.”

00:13:40.651 --> 00:13:41.651
“What!

00:13:41.651 --> 00:13:42.651
You got a kid?”

00:13:42.651 --> 00:13:43.651
“Yeah…”

00:13:43.651 --> 00:13:44.680
“Way to beat the odds, soldier.”

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:47.500
Recall the way Gabby Gabby reads her book
like a bible.

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:52.250
Similarly, Duke spends all his time bemoaning
the way he let down his former child owner,

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:56.601
and when he performs a life-threatening stunt,
it is his child’s face that appears to him,

00:13:56.601 --> 00:13:58.161
like a divine mirage.

00:13:58.161 --> 00:14:03.980
In this context, Bo Peep has committed the
ultimate sin: she’s rejected children, and

00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:06.281
in doing so, rejected God.

00:14:06.281 --> 00:14:11.190
In John Milton’s famous epic Paradise Lost,
a prideful Satan is depicted as being cast

00:14:11.190 --> 00:14:15.011
out from heaven and condemned to Hell after
a failed rebellion against God.

00:14:15.011 --> 00:14:19.531
He didn’t accept God’s dominion over him,
and thought all angels should rule as Gods

00:14:19.531 --> 00:14:20.531
themselves.

00:14:20.531 --> 00:14:25.320
Similarly, Bo Peep didn’t want to belong
to a child anymore, she wanted to be her own

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:26.320
master.

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:30.380
When she and Woody reunite after years, he
assumes that she must be devastated not to

00:14:30.380 --> 00:14:31.401
have a child.

00:14:31.401 --> 00:14:34.461
Instead, she seems free, almost ecstatic.

00:14:34.461 --> 00:14:38.961
Perhaps fittingly, moments earlier, the pair
fall down a hill - Is it a stretch to see

00:14:38.961 --> 00:14:44.031
this then, as Woody’ satanic-like “fall”
from his role as the eternally devout toy?

00:14:44.031 --> 00:14:45.730
Hmm… maybe.

00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:51.430
There’s more though: Satan is practically
defined by his predilections as a tempter.

00:14:51.430 --> 00:14:56.350
In Goethe’s Faust, a devil tries to convince
Faust to sell his soul in exchange for all

00:14:56.350 --> 00:15:00.661
worldly pleasures, telling him to:

00:15:00.661 --> 00:15:07.671
Similarly, Bo Peep lures Woody away from the
quiet, safe world he has known all his life.

00:15:07.671 --> 00:15:12.771
She embodies temptation, compelling Woody
with promises of exciting carnivals and far-flung

00:15:12.771 --> 00:15:13.771
lands.

00:15:13.771 --> 00:15:18.000
If we do take this interpretation seriously,
it’s also worth noting the fun reversal

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:22.690
of traditional Christian imagery - Bo Peep
is a shepherd, just like Jesus… except she’s

00:15:22.690 --> 00:15:24.461
leading toys away from the flock.

00:15:24.461 --> 00:15:29.581
She’s really the anti-shepherd encouraging
everyone to go their own way in life, free

00:15:29.581 --> 00:15:33.531
of the responsibility they all feel to their
sometimes-unappreciative children.

00:15:33.531 --> 00:15:37.790
Now before you raise your eyebrows too high,
know that we’d only grade this interpretation

00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:38.790
a square C+.

00:15:38.790 --> 00:15:43.261
If you can’t quite picture Bo Peep wearing
devil horns, that’s ok.

00:15:43.261 --> 00:15:45.591
We’ve got one more interpretation for you.

00:15:45.591 --> 00:15:50.130
We see some definite psychological complexes
exhibited by Forky and it helps explain why

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:52.310
he desperately wants to bathe in trash.

00:15:52.310 --> 00:15:55.711
To understand this, let’s look at Sigmund
Freud and Otto Rank.

00:15:55.711 --> 00:16:00.730
Now, Freud describes birth, or leaving the
womb, as the "first experience of anxiety

00:16:00.730 --> 00:16:03.250
and thus the source and prototype of ... anxiety.”

00:16:03.250 --> 00:16:08.410
His one-time colleague Otto Rank took it further,
suggesting that birth isn’t just anxiety-inducing…

00:16:08.410 --> 00:16:12.701
it’s actually traumatic, the greatest trauma
of life, in fact.

00:16:12.701 --> 00:16:16.930
Think about it - you go from the reasonably
chill, quiet world of your mom’s womb (sorry)

00:16:16.930 --> 00:16:20.610
to a weird outside world where your mom’s
screaming, fluorescent lights are shining

00:16:20.610 --> 00:16:22.420
and then some doctor spanks your butt.

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:24.931
Not the kind of activity you want to repeat.

00:16:24.931 --> 00:16:28.931
So now consider that Forky’s “birth”
of sorts took place in the kindergarten classroom

00:16:28.931 --> 00:16:32.841
when he was clumsily pieced together by Bonnie
from disparate pieces of trash.

00:16:32.841 --> 00:16:36.211
As indicated by the first noise he makes when
he becomes vocal,

00:16:36.211 --> 00:16:37.211
“Ahhhhhhh!”

00:16:37.211 --> 00:16:40.391
Forky’s birth seems to have been at least
a little traumatic.

00:16:40.391 --> 00:16:44.480
Rank notes that the resulting trauma from
birth leads us to long for a return to our

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:49.810
primal state, noting that “every pleasure
has as its final aim the re-establishment

00:16:49.810 --> 00:16:51.951
of the intrauterine primal pleasure.”

00:16:51.951 --> 00:16:56.331
Basically, we have a desire to return to the
womb, even though we definitely would not

00:16:56.331 --> 00:16:57.331
fit anymore.

00:16:57.331 --> 00:17:00.781
As a result, we experience infantile regression,
which means that we’ll seek out replacements

00:17:00.781 --> 00:17:04.860
for our mom’s womb in various objects or
other people, which a Freudian might say is

00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:07.391
the reason a Freudian might say is the reason
you enjoy jacuzzis, sorry.

00:17:07.391 --> 00:17:12.301
Anyway, we see some hardcore infantile regression
coming from Forky, who desperately seeks to

00:17:12.301 --> 00:17:15.210
return to his own “womb,” which is to
say, the trash.

00:17:15.210 --> 00:17:19.200
In what becomes one of the film’s funniest
bits, Forky gleefully throws himself into

00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:20.850
every trash can he can find.

00:17:20.850 --> 00:17:24.411
He reverently calls out for trash the way
a child might call out for their own mother,

00:17:24.411 --> 00:17:27.491
and exhibits the same kind of clingy longing
that a newborn might.

00:17:27.491 --> 00:17:31.181
When Woody asks him why he’s so obsessed
with trash cans, he gives an answer that would

00:17:31.181 --> 00:17:32.961
certainly raise Otto Rank’s eyebrow:

00:17:32.961 --> 00:17:33.961
“It’s warm…”

00:17:33.961 --> 00:17:34.961
“Ew.”

00:17:34.961 --> 00:17:35.961
“And cozy…”

00:17:35.961 --> 00:17:36.961
“I guess.”

00:17:36.961 --> 00:17:37.961
“And safe!

00:17:37.961 --> 00:17:40.661
Like somebody’s whispering in your ear ‘Everything’s
gonna be okay.’”

00:17:40.661 --> 00:17:44.151
If that’s not infantile regression, I don’t
know what is!

00:17:44.151 --> 00:17:48.611
Okay but real talk, we’ll give Forky’s
garbage womb interpretation a solid C.

00:17:48.611 --> 00:17:53.541
Now, whether or not you buy any of these ways
of reading the film, I think we can all agree

00:17:53.541 --> 00:17:58.801
that Pixar created another extremely thoughtful,
emotionally powerful film with Toy Story 4.

00:17:58.801 --> 00:18:02.911
And at least for our favorite reading, it
seems like the geniuses at Pixar have created

00:18:02.911 --> 00:18:08.141
a toy-sized mirror for some of the anxieties
we may be facing in an increasingly secular

00:18:08.141 --> 00:18:09.141
world.

00:18:09.141 --> 00:18:10.141
But what do you guys think?

00:18:10.141 --> 00:18:13.911
Do you buy that Toy Story is an existentialist
saga, or do you think it’s more in line

00:18:13.911 --> 00:18:17.220
with our not-so-secret desire to crawl back
into a nice big womb?

00:18:17.220 --> 00:18:19.141
Let us know in the comments.

00:18:19.141 --> 00:18:23.311
As ever, a big ole thanks to our wonderful
patrons who sponsor our show and podcasts.

00:18:23.311 --> 00:18:53.720
Be sure to click that subscribe button and,
as always, peace!

